                            <a name="comment239"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Dear Mr. Kramer
</h4>

<p>
Did you really just write "might could"? And I only thought that was acceptable at my redneck high school. Then you have Kelley advertising for some hot wings. Clearly, y'all have got to come to Memphis for the first annual Gangrey-i-con.<br />
<br />
Myself a three-year Nieman veteran, I have to say I thought they hit just the right balance this year, between the sessions devoted to craft and those meant more for inspiration. Jacqui, our narrative Mick Jagger, of course gave us lots of both. Even Jamaica Kincaid, who does things I do not imagine I will ever attempt, gave me something because she talked about exploring that place where her muse lived. <br />
<br />
I actually would be wary of a session devoted too much to the 'hazards' of our every-day existence, because I know how easily such things can devolve into outright bitch sessions. Kelley's right, and Dianna Sugg brought this home, too -- nobody is preventing any of us from doing extraordinary journalism (other than the bastard corporations, I mean). I need Nieman for the pure oxygen it provides, and though I'm well aware most of us operate in environments with varying degrees of pollution, I kind of like three days where I'm thinking mostly about what is possible and less about all the obstacles -- real and imagined -- in the way. 
</p>
